## HARD — Dialogue

**Plain talk.** Normal sentences. No epigrams, thesis lines, title-echo farewells, inventory stubs.

**Engage.** Not plot-handoff teleports. People interrupt, ask wrong follow-ups, refuse partway, bargain, answer sideways. Important info lands through a real exchange.

**Lean (match the live canon pass, Ch 5–10):**

| Do | Don't |
|---|---|
| One clean hit per beat (esp. Riku) | Extra clause that explains the same thing twice |
| NPCs in full sentences | Charm-stub summaries |
| Plain closes ("The day isn't over." / "Come on.") | Title-echo buttons ("Tuesday isn't over.") |
| Attribute, then move | Narrator garnish on every line ("filing a small improvement…") |
| Put the object in the sentence | Dangling "maybe not" / "after that" with no referent |
| Cut thank-you / "don't thank me" ping-pong | Soft-boy lecture spam; "ask soft" / bulletin rule cards |

When the user rewrites dialogue, **that pass is canon voice** for the next chapters.

---

## HARD — Narrator is a person, not a record (removed device — do not reintroduce)

The book is plain first-person narration. There is **no frame, no listener, no written "record."**

### Tense (locked)

| Stretch | Tense |
|---|---|
| Ch 1 → arrival at Sugi's door (Ch 4) | **Past** |
| Nagihama onward (Ch 4 after the door →) | **Present** |

- Live the scene *now*. Do not slip into past for action that is happening ("I dropped my shoulders" → "I drop my shoulders").
- **No future-memory narration.** Ban: "I will carry this forever," "I will recognize it in my sleep," "That sentence will matter later." Character plans in present are fine ("I'm going to Kuroishi," "I'll help"). Prophetic *will* about what the story means later is not.
- Dialogue may use any natural tense. The ban is on narrator voice.

### No filing / no ledger voice

- **Banned as narration or thought:** record, ledger, column, entry, account (as metaphor), "filed under," "closes files," "file behind his eyes," "voice like a receipt," "seating chart" for people, arithmetic-as-feeling ("did the math," "the sum ran," "totals itself," coin/loud *cost* metaphors as habit).
- **Banned: mental-collection framing.** The narrator does not "keep," "collect," or "store" observations ("I kept it with the others," "I've been collecting these"). He notices and moves on.
- **Banned:** reader/listener address ("I want you to see," "I'll give it to you"). Generic idiomatic *you* is fine.
- **Allowed:** rare quiet hindsight with no record talk — at most one or two per chapter, at real hinges.
- **Literal paper stays:** Orihara's shop books, Sato's stamps, FDC files, official accounts. The ban is narrator metaphor, not the world's paper.

### Clean dialogue (quick check)

Full sentences. No telegram stubs. No epigrams / thesis farewells. People interrupt, refuse, bargain. One clean hit per beat — no double-explain.
Real questions take `?` — "You've been to Takamatsu?" / "What kind of work?" Periods on questions only when the line is deliberately not a question (Ch 5: `"Can you." Not a question.`). Do not default every ask to a flat period.

## HARD — Narration: story prose, not poetry

**North star:** `assets/example-chapter-1`.

- Cause → action → result. Reader follows without decoding.
- Longer clear beats over stacked one-line images.
- Detail on the task (who, where, why the next move changes) — not pretty weather.
- Feeling only when short and attached to a choice.
- Cut on action, speech, or next step — never on a literary button.
- Bad: "The road forgets the harbor. Scrapyard fence. Still walking."
- Bad (thesis / first-time buttons): "for the first time since Nagihama the road behind us has a city on it instead of dust" / "That, somehow, is the kindest thing anyone has done for me since…" / "Somewhere ahead the road keeps going… and the only honest thing left in my mouth…" — if the line could be a chapter blurb, cut it; end on what they do next.
- Good: "I already used Sato's stamp at the first checkpoint, so this one I have to talk through." / "I walk into the yards and join the line of boys waiting for the afternoon load."

### HARD — No personified places / no metaphor-road closes

Places do not wake, forget, breathe meaning, or moralize. Roads do not stand in for fate. End on concrete next moves — who shouts, which cut, what hurts — not atmosphere posing as a close.

- Banned: "the hospital is waking up wrong" / "the city holds its breath" / "the alley remembers" / "whatever road she has after that" / "ahead is only the dark and what we become"
- Allowed: "someone is shouting in the service court" / "the drainage cut runs to the pine road" / "my shoulder burns where the fence cut it"
- Same ban on chapter/section exits: if the last paragraph could be a trailer line, rewrite it as what they do next.

