## HARD — Craft bar (from the example study)

Canon references: `assets/example-style-guide.md` (what good looks like, with quoted exemplars) and `assets/current-chapters-critique.md` (our known gaps, ranked). The rules above still bind; these close the gap between "clean" and "as good as the examples."

### Dialogue is a transaction

- Before writing an exchange, know what each side wants and what each side pays — work, coin, embarrassment, a favor owed, a lie they must maintain later. If one side wants nothing, the scene is a lecture: rewrite or cut.
- Information arrives bent by the teller's interest — partial, self-serving, or wrong on the first pass. No NPC hands over exactly what the plot needs after one token gruffness.
- At least one refusal per arc **stays refused**. Not every ask lands.
- Wisdom never announces itself as a gift ("that one's free"). It leaks sideways from self-interest.
- Chains of consecutive info-yielding NPCs are banned (the Ch 13 problem). If three strangers must feed the trail, at least one misleads, sandbags, or wants something back.

### One full complication per chapter

- Every chapter dramatizes at least one scene where the plan meets friction on-page and gets improvised around. Summary compresses **time**, never **conflict**.
- No purely connective chapters (transit + work montage; the Ch 12 problem). If a chapter only moves bodies between cities, fold it into its neighbors or build a real scene inside it.
- A scene that ends with everyone knowing and wanting what they did at the start is a stall — turn it or cut it.

### Echo budget — no-re-teach extends to lines and themes

- Signature gestures (tide-chart face, tongue-click, crate-hands), thesis lines ("nobody is coming to fix this"), and carried sentences (the six syllables) get **one use per POV block**. Each recurrence must add, invert, or get contradicted — or be cut.
- Cost tells never run the same sequence/phrasing twice (copper → lip blood → "weight leaves my throat" is a stamp). Vary order, body part, aftermath.
- Before drafting, check the previous two chapters for close type and motifs used; repeat neither.

### Wrong reads are mandatory

- Per POV block, the lead makes one **confident read that is wrong**, and the bill arrives on-page (model: the wrong boy at the docks, Ch 16).
- Every camera crossing exploits dramatic irony: the reader knows from the other block what this camera is misreading. Never waste a crossing on agreement.
- Recurring allies are fallible. Mira (and any steerer) has visible wants of her own and at least one costly miscall per volume. A competence dispenser is as banned as a food dispenser.

### NPC register variety

- One governing attitude per NPC, every line runs through it — and the attitude comes off the **unused** shelf. The gruff-feeding-auntie register is retired (Sugi, Orihara, Orii, Machi already own it). Next helpers help out of vanity, boredom, gossip value, or while being genuinely unpleasant; let the boys owe someone they dislike.
- One load-bearing detail per NPC — a detail that characterizes now and pays off later. No inventory description.

### Close rotation

- Chapter closes rotate hook types: decision, reveal, threat, new arrival, question with stakes. Never the same type twice running. The quiet-exit / keep-walking close is capped at **once per POV block** (a volume close may earn it — only if the chapters before it didn't spend the chord).
- The last line is the scene's turn crystallized — earned in the scene, not appended.

### Leads carry their own thread

- Every Haruki chapter moves **his** problem one notch — the too-useful voice working too well on someone, the unpaid parts of home, what he will actually do when he catches up — not just trail progress. Every Kaito chapter moves his — the mouth, Mira, what he is becoming. A chapter that only advances the hunt is not done.

---

## When unsure

Prefer silence, partial understanding, or wrong conclusions over a convenient dump. Writer truth can wait; character truth cannot be faked.
